  1173→\paragraph{Summary.}
  1174→The comparison reveals complementary strengths. On the oscillatory Oregonator, flux-adaptive time stepping provides a decisive advantage by automatically scaling $\Delta t$ to the instantaneous activity level, avoiding both the state space explosion and the computational bottleneck of fixed small steps. On the bottleneck system, flux-based pruning preserves essential connectivity that probability-only pruning destroys, producing qualitatively correct dynamics where the baseline method fails. On the short-horizon Robertson system, the Krylov matrix exponential allows larger steps and the baseline is more efficient; however, the flux-adaptive method provides greater robustness on longer time horizons where the accept/reject mechanism degrades. These results demonstrate that flux-based pruning and time stepping provide robustness advantages on multiscale systems with bottlenecks or oscillatory dynamics, while the methods perform comparably on systems without severe connectivity or stiffness challenges.
