\paragraph{Summary.}
The comparison reveals complementary strengths. On the oscillatory Oregonator, flux-adaptive time stepping provides a decisive advantage by automatically scaling $\Delta t$ to the instantaneous activity level, avoiding both the state space explosion and the computational bottleneck of fixed small steps. On the bottleneck system, flux-based pruning preserves essential connectivity that probability-only pruning destroys, producing qualitatively correct dynamics where the baseline method fails. On the short-horizon Robertson system, the Krylov matrix exponential allows larger steps and the baseline is more efficient; however, the flux-adaptive method provides greater robustness on longer time horizons where the accept/reject mechanism degrades. These results demonstrate that flux-based pruning and time stepping provide robustness advantages on multiscale systems with bottlenecks or oscillatory dynamics, while the methods perform comparably on systems without severe connectivity or stiffness challenges.

\subsection{Efficiency Under Corrected Krylov-FSP-SSA Parameters}
\label{subsec:corrected_efficiency}

A natural question is whether the efficiency advantages in Tables~\ref{tab:comparison_oregonator}--\ref{tab:comparison_robertson} hold when Krylov-FSP-SSA parameters are tuned to produce \emph{correct} results. Table~\ref{tab:corrected_efficiency} summarises this comparison.

\textbf{Oregonator.} The $\tau$-lock at $\tau \approx 10^{-6}$ arises because the Krylov matrix exponential loses mass conservation for any larger step, owing to the large individual propensities ($k_3 X Z \sim 10^5$). This is independent of $\varepsilon$: tighter pruning reduces $|\mathcal{S}|$ but does not lower the propensity of the surviving states. Krylov-FSP-SSA therefore cannot complete $t_f = 0.11$ at any practical efficiency, regardless of parameter tuning.

\textbf{Bottleneck.} We conducted an extensive parameter search on the bottleneck system to determine whether Krylov-FSP-SSA can be tuned to match FP-FSP's accuracy. The core difficulty is that the distribution of $C$ given $B{=}1$ is not concentrated at $\langle C \rangle \approx 5$: the bottleneck transition $A \to B$ occurs uniformly over $[0, t_f]$, so $C \mid B{=}1$ is spread approximately uniformly from $0$ to $k_2 t_f = 1000$, with mean $\approx 500$ and standard deviation $\approx 577$. The full probability wavefront therefore extends from $C = 0$ to $C \approx 1000$, even though $\langle C \rangle = 5$.

Krylov-FSP-SSA retains a state only if its probability exceeds $\varepsilon/|\mathcal{S}|$. States on the wavefront at $C \approx k_2 t$ have near-zero probability (probability mass has not yet flowed there) but carry significant outgoing flux $k_2 p(B{=}1, C{=}c)$. They are therefore systematically pruned by probability thresholding, regardless of how $\varepsilon$ is set: small $\varepsilon$ tightens the pruning threshold and retains more wavefront states, but simultaneously tightens the mass-conservation acceptance criterion, triggering $\tau$-halving when boundary absorption becomes non-negligible; large $\varepsilon$ prevents $\tau$-halving but allows too much boundary absorption at the wavefront per step. This is a fundamental conflict with no single-$\varepsilon$ resolution.

Empirically, tuning $(\varepsilon, \tau, r)$ over a wide range (Table~\ref{tab:corrected_efficiency}) achieves at best $\langle C \rangle \approx 4.72$ ($\sim$5\% error) in 0.9\,s --- comparable wall time to FP-FSP but with $50\times$ greater relative error. The flux-based method retains wavefront states because their outgoing flux $k_2 p(B{=}1, C{=}c)$ is large relative to the total flux, independent of their probability. This is precisely the intended role of flux-based pruning: it correctly identifies and preserves states that are \emph{dynamically important} even when they are probabilistically rare.

\textbf{Robertson ($t_f = 10^4$).} Krylov-FSP-SSA reaches $t = 50$ in 500 iterations at $\tau = 0.1$ (0.5\% complete), requiring an extrapolated $10^5$ iterations to finish $t_f = 10^4$. The initial $\tau$-halving cascade observed in Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison} forces small steps near $t = 0$; once the fast transient passes, $\tau$ recovers to $0.1$ but the step size is insufficient to cover the full horizon within a practical iteration budget. FP-FSP succeeds because flux-adaptive $\Delta t$ spans six orders of magnitude as the system transitions between fast transients and slow equilibration.

