\subsection{Comparison with Krylov-FSP-SSA}
\label{sec:comparison}

To contextualize the flux-based adaptive FSP against existing methods, we compare with the Krylov-FSP-SSA algorithm of Vo and Sidje~\cite{Sidje2015}, reviewed in detail by Dinh and Sidje~\cite{Dinh2016}. This baseline shares the same expand--evolve--prune skeleton and Krylov matrix exponential integrator but differs in three key respects: (i) pruning uses a hard probability threshold with no flux protection, (ii) time stepping uses a mass-conservation accept/reject criterion (halving $\tau$ when the retained probability mass falls below $1 - \varepsilon$) rather than the flux-adaptive rule $\Delta t = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$, and (iii) expansion is SSA-driven with $r$-step reachability enumeration. These differences isolate the effect of flux-aware pruning and flux-adaptive time stepping on the three benchmark systems.

\paragraph{Oregonator.}
Table~\ref{tab:comparison_oregonator} and Figure~\ref{fig:oregonator_comparison} compare the two methods on the Oregonator system over $[0, 0.11]$.
The flux-based adaptive FSP completes the simulation in 37\,s using 28 snapshots with a maximum active set of 1{,}063 states, while the Krylov-FSP-SSA method, using a fixed step $\tau = 10^{-6}$ (the largest step that avoids numerical overflow in the Krylov exponential), reaches only $t = 0.003$ (2.7\% of the time horizon) after 3{,}000 iterations and 103\,s of wall time, with an active set that grows to 13{,}299 states. The state space explosion (panel~(a)) arises because the probability-only pruning threshold ($\varepsilon = 10^{-2}$) retains many states with small probability that the flux-based rule would prune, while failing to identify the dynamically essential boundary states. The time step comparison (panel~(b)) illustrates the core advantage of flux-adaptive stepping: the adaptive FSP adjusts $\Delta t$ over two orders of magnitude ($10^{-6}$ to $10^{-4}$) in response to the instantaneous total flux, whereas the Krylov-FSP-SSA method is locked at $\tau = 10^{-6}$ because larger steps cause mass loss exceeding the tolerance.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\caption{Oregonator comparison: flux-based adaptive FSP vs.\ Krylov-FSP-SSA.}
\label{tab:comparison_oregonator}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
Metric & Adaptive FSP & Krylov-FSP-SSA \\
\hline
Wall time (s) & 37 & 103 \\
Snapshots / Iterations & 28 & 3{,}000 \\
Time reached & 0.11 (complete) & 0.003 (2.7\%) \\
Max $|S|$ & 1{,}063 & 13{,}299 \\
Final $|S|$ & 1{,}034 & 13{,}299 \\
Rejections & --- & 0 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{oregonator_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Oregonator: FP-FSP (solid blue) vs.\ Krylov-FSP-SSA (dashed red). (a) State space size: Krylov-FSP-SSA grows to $\sim$13{,}300 states without flux-based pruning, while FP-FSP maintains $<$1{,}100. (b) Time step: FP-FSP adjusts $\Delta t$ over two orders of magnitude; Krylov-FSP-SSA is fixed at $\tau = 10^{-6}$.}
    \label{fig:oregonator_comparison}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Bottleneck.}
Table~\ref{tab:comparison_bottleneck} and Figure~\ref{fig:bottleneck_comparison} compare the methods on the bottleneck system ($A \to B \to B + C$, $k_1 = 10^{-6}$, $k_2 = 0.1$) over $[0, 10^4]$. This system provides the clearest demonstration of connectivity preservation. The flux-based adaptive FSP uses fixed steps $\delta t = 10$ (1{,}000 iterations), completes in 0.4\,s, and achieves $\langle C \rangle = 4.97$ at the final time. The Krylov-FSP-SSA method completes in $<0.1$\,s (100 iterations, $\tau = 100$) but produces $\langle C \rangle \approx 0$, completely missing~$C$ production.

The discrepancy arises because after a single step of $\tau = 100$\,s, the bottleneck state $(0, 1, 0)$ carries probability $p(B{=}1) \approx k_1 \tau = 10^{-4}$, well below the Krylov-FSP-SSA pruning threshold $\varepsilon/|\mathcal{S}| \approx 10^{-2}/4 \approx 2.5 \times 10^{-3}$. Without flux protection, this state is pruned every iteration, severing the only pathway through which probability can flow from the $A = 1$ initial condition to the $C$-producing reaction $B \to B + C$. The flux-based method retains $(0, 1, c)$ because its outgoing flux $p(B{=}1) \times k_2$ is large relative to the total system flux, despite the low probability.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\caption{Bottleneck comparison: connectivity preservation under flux-based vs.\ probability-only pruning.}
\label{tab:comparison_bottleneck}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
Metric & Adaptive FSP & Krylov-FSP-SSA \\
\hline
Wall time (s) & 0.4 & ${<}0.1$ \\
Steps / Iterations & 1{,}000 ($\delta t=10$) & 100 ($\tau=100$, $\varepsilon=10^{-2}$) \\
Time reached & $10^4$ (complete) & $10^4$ (complete) \\
Final $\langle C \rangle$ & 4.97 & ${\approx}0$ \\
Final $|S|$ & 1{,}001 & 4 \\
$B \geq 1$ state retained & Yes & No \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{bottleneck_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Bottleneck system: FP-FSP (solid blue) vs.\ Krylov-FSP-SSA (dashed red). (a) Mean $\langle C \rangle$ trajectory: FP-FSP tracks the reference growth of species~$C$, while Krylov-FSP-SSA underestimates production after connectivity is lost. (b) Mean $\langle B \rangle$ trajectory.}
    \label{fig:bottleneck_comparison}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Robertson.}
Table~\ref{tab:comparison_robertson} and Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison} report the Robertson system comparison over $[0, 10]$. Here both methods complete the simulation, but with markedly different computational costs. The flux-based adaptive FSP requires 877\,s due to the small time steps ($\Delta t \sim 1.3 \times 10^{-5}$) needed to track the stiff dynamics at the flux-adaptive scale. The Krylov-FSP-SSA method completes in 0.02\,s using 101 iterations with large accepted steps, because the matrix exponential integrator can take steps exceeding the fastest reaction timescale without losing mass conservation. This result is expected: on a short time horizon without bottleneck states, the accept/reject time stepping of Krylov-FSP-SSA is competitive, and the flux-adaptive rule pays a cost for resolving transient dynamics at finer temporal resolution. Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison} shows the initial $\tau$ halving cascade---from $\tau_{\mathrm{init}} = 0.1$ down to $\sim 10^{-14}$---followed by rapid recovery once the matrix exponential absorbs the fast transient.

However, extending the Robertson system to longer horizons ($t_f = 10^4$) reveals the limitations of the accept/reject approach: starting from $\tau_{\mathrm{init}} = 0.1$, repeated halving is required to satisfy the mass conservation criterion (because the total propensity $\sim 3 \times 10^6$ demands $\tau \ll 10^{-6}$ for stability), and the method makes negligible progress toward $t_f$. Meanwhile, the flux-adaptive method automatically adjusts $\Delta t$ across six orders of magnitude as the system transitions between fast transients and slow equilibration.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\caption{Robertson comparison ($t_f = 10$): both methods complete, with different cost profiles.}
\label{tab:comparison_robertson}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
Metric & Adaptive FSP & Krylov-FSP-SSA \\
\hline
Wall time (s) & 877 & 0.02 \\
Total iterations & 147 & 101 \\
Time reached & 10.0 (complete) & 10.0 (complete) \\
Rejections & --- & 0 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{robertson_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Robertson system: Krylov-FSP-SSA time step $\tau$ vs.\ iteration, showing the initial halving cascade from $\tau_{\mathrm{init}} = 10^{-1}$ down to ${\sim}10^{-14}$, followed by rapid recovery once the matrix exponential absorbs the fast transient.}
    \label{fig:robertson_comparison}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Summary.}
The comparison reveals complementary strengths. On the oscillatory Oregonator, flux-adaptive time stepping provides a decisive advantage by automatically scaling $\Delta t$ to the instantaneous activity level, avoiding both the state space explosion and the computational bottleneck of fixed small steps. On the bottleneck system, flux-based pruning preserves essential connectivity that probability-only pruning destroys, producing qualitatively correct dynamics where the baseline method fails. On the short-horizon Robertson system, the Krylov matrix exponential allows larger steps and the baseline is more efficient; however, the flux-adaptive method provides greater robustness on longer time horizons where the accept/reject mechanism degrades. These results demonstrate that flux-based pruning and time stepping provide robustness advantages on multiscale systems with bottlenecks or oscillatory dynamics, while the methods perform comparably on systems without severe connectivity or stiffness challenges.

\subsection{Efficiency Under Corrected Krylov-FSP-SSA Parameters}
\label{subsec:corrected_efficiency}

A natural question is whether the efficiency advantages in Tables~\ref{tab:comparison_oregonator}--\ref{tab:comparison_robertson} hold when Krylov-FSP-SSA parameters are tuned to produce \emph{correct} results. Table~\ref{tab:corrected_efficiency} summarises this comparison.

\textbf{Oregonator.} The $\tau$-lock at $\tau \approx 10^{-6}$ arises because the Krylov matrix exponential loses mass conservation for any larger step, owing to the large individual propensities ($k_3 X Z \sim 10^5$). This is independent of $\varepsilon$: tighter pruning reduces $|\mathcal{S}|$ but does not lower the propensity of the surviving states. Krylov-FSP-SSA therefore cannot complete $t_f = 0.11$ at any practical efficiency, regardless of parameter tuning.

\textbf{Bottleneck.} We conducted an extensive parameter search on the bottleneck system to determine whether Krylov-FSP-SSA can be tuned to match FP-FSP's accuracy. The core difficulty is that the distribution of $C$ given $B{=}1$ is not concentrated at $\langle C \rangle \approx 5$: the bottleneck transition $A \to B$ occurs uniformly over $[0, t_f]$, so $C \mid B{=}1$ is spread approximately uniformly from $0$ to $k_2 t_f = 1000$, with mean $\approx 500$ and standard deviation $\approx 577$. The full probability wavefront therefore extends from $C = 0$ to $C \approx 1000$, even though $\langle C \rangle = 5$.

Krylov-FSP-SSA retains a state only if its probability exceeds $\varepsilon/|\mathcal{S}|$. States on the wavefront at $C \approx k_2 t$ have near-zero probability (probability mass has not yet flowed there) but carry significant outgoing flux $k_2 p(B{=}1, C{=}c)$. They are therefore systematically pruned by probability thresholding, regardless of how $\varepsilon$ is set: small $\varepsilon$ tightens the pruning threshold and retains more wavefront states, but simultaneously tightens the mass-conservation acceptance criterion, triggering $\tau$-halving when boundary absorption becomes non-negligible; large $\varepsilon$ prevents $\tau$-halving but allows too much boundary absorption at the wavefront per step. This is a fundamental conflict with no single-$\varepsilon$ resolution.

Empirically, tuning $(\varepsilon, \tau, r)$ over a wide range (Table~\ref{tab:corrected_efficiency}) achieves at best $\langle C \rangle \approx 4.72$ ($\sim$5\% error) in 0.9\,s --- comparable wall time to FP-FSP but with $50\times$ greater relative error. The flux-based method retains wavefront states because their outgoing flux $k_2 p(B{=}1, C{=}c)$ is large relative to the total flux, independent of their probability. This is precisely the intended role of flux-based pruning: it correctly identifies and preserves states that are \emph{dynamically important} even when they are probabilistically rare.

\textbf{Robertson ($t_f = 10^4$).} Krylov-FSP-SSA reaches $t = 50$ in 500 iterations at $\tau = 0.1$ (0.5\% complete), requiring an extrapolated $10^5$ iterations to finish $t_f = 10^4$. The initial $\tau$-halving cascade observed in Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison} forces small steps near $t = 0$; once the fast transient passes, $\tau$ recovers to $0.1$ but the step size is insufficient to cover the full horizon within a practical iteration budget. FP-FSP succeeds because flux-adaptive $\Delta t$ spans six orders of magnitude as the system transitions between fast transients and slow equilibration.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\caption{Efficiency with corrected Krylov-FSP-SSA parameters on the bottleneck system ($t_f = 10^4$). Best result over a sweep of $(\varepsilon, \tau, r)$ is $\langle C \rangle \approx 4.72$ ($\sim$5\% error) at comparable wall time to FP-FSP ($\langle C \rangle = 4.98$, ${<}0.1\%$ error). FP-FSP times for other systems from Tables~\ref{tab:comparison_oregonator}--\ref{tab:comparison_robertson}.}
\label{tab:corrected_efficiency}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\hline
System & FP-FSP & KrylovFSP (default) & KrylovFSP (best tuned) & Verdict \\
\hline
Oregonator & 37\,s & 103\,s, 2.7\% & DNF ($\tau$-lock, all $\varepsilon$) & FP-FSP wins \\
Bottleneck & 0.4\,s & ${<}0.1$\,s, $\langle C\rangle{\approx}0$ & 0.9\,s, $\langle C\rangle{\approx}4.72$ (${\sim}5\%$ err) & FP-FSP: ${<}0.1\%$ err \\
Robertson $t_f{=}10^4$ & ${>}877$\,s (est.) & DNF (0.5\%) & DNF (0.5\%, same) & FP-FSP survives \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The key finding is that the failure modes are qualitatively distinct across all three systems. On the Oregonator, the $\tau$-lock is intrinsic to the mass-conservation criterion and cannot be resolved by any parameter choice. On the bottleneck system, any single value of $\varepsilon$ creates a fundamental conflict between retaining wavefront states (requires small $\varepsilon$) and preventing excessive boundary absorption (requires large $\varepsilon$); the best achievable accuracy is $\sim$5\% error regardless of the parameter combination. On long-horizon stiff systems, the accept/reject step-size mechanism cannot cover the full horizon within a practical iteration budget. In all three cases, the flux-based adaptive method provides correct results under default parameters, because flux-based pruning retains dynamically important states regardless of their probability, and flux-adaptive time stepping automatically matches the step size to the instantaneous activity level.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

